    "event_confirmation_state": "CONFIRMED",
    "event_proposition": "Futures markets allow commodities producers and consumers to engage in “hedging” in order to limit the risk of losing money as commodity prices change - For example, a Kansas wheat farmer who plants a crop runs the risk of losing money if the price of wheat falls before harvest and sale - The farmer can minimize this risk by selling wheat futures contracts, which guarantee that the farmer will receive a predetermined price - Hedging helps the economy function by allowing commodities producers (such as farmers) and consumers (such as millers) to conduct their businesses with greater certainty over how much they can expect to earn from and pay for commodities",
